Overtaking planning and decision-making are central to performance in autonomous racing.
Limited maneuvering space on narrow closed circuits makes high-speed overtaking difficult and increases the risk of failed passes and collisions.
To address this problem, we formulate interactive overtaking as robust tactical feasible-set selection and develop a game-guided decision framework that distils Stackelberg response-set reasoning into persistent tactical corridors for constrained motion planning and closed-loop execution.
First, a robust Stackelberg response-set teacher provides action-prior and action-conditioned robust-value supervision to a Truncated Quantile Critics (TQC) policy that learns passing-side intention, corridor parameters, and speed demand.
Second, a hysteretic state machine preserves the selected passing side, while a deterministic corridor carver combines the learned corridor parameters with the evolving track and interaction geometry to construct a time-varying Frenet corridor.
Finally, a corridor-constrained sequential SOCP planner and speed profiler generate a continuous path–speed reference, which is tracked by a reference-conditioned contouring-error nonlinear model predictive controller (RC-NMPC) for closed-loop vehicle control.
Hardware-in-the-loop experiments were conducted using an EAV24 formula race-car simulator. Across 1000 laps, the method achieved an overtaking success rate of 94.7\% and a mean overtaking time of 4.82 s. Compared with four baselines using the same execution stack, the success rate was 31.2–66.2 percentage points higher and the mean overtaking time was 2.43–6.41 s shorter; no collisions or track-boundary violations were observed.

